@@ -1,8 +1,30 @@
 # stock-sync
 
 Consumes `stock.changed` events from the message broker and pushes stock levels to the
-marketplace partner's inventory API. After each successful send it publishes the sent item
-to `stock.sent` (the warehouse loads that topic for the "what did we send" report).
+marketplace partner's inventory API in batches. After each successful send it publishes the
+sent items to `stock.sent` (the warehouse loads that topic for the "what did we send" report).
+
+## Batching
+
+Items are buffered (`stock_sync/batcher.py`) and sent when either condition is met:
+
+- `BATCH_MIN_ITEMS` (25) items are buffered — the partner's requested minimum per request;
+- the oldest buffered item reaches `BATCH_MAX_AGE_SECONDS` (10 min) — the partner needs
+  stock within 15 minutes of the change, so the deadline wins and we send a short batch.
+
+Requests are split at `BATCH_MAX_ITEMS` (500), the partner's per-call limit. At current
+traffic (~6k events/day over 8 pods) most batches are deadline-triggered and hold fewer
+than 25 items; the minimum is only reachable inside 15 minutes during busy spells.
+
+Two other things the batching relies on:
+
+- **Offsets are committed only after the batch has been sent**, so a crash re-delivers the
+  buffered events rather than losing them. The sent-log still makes re-delivery a no-op.
+- **The broker client must yield `None` when a poll times out**, so `consumer.run` gets a
+  chance to flush during quiet hours when no event arrives.
+
+At most one item per SKU goes into a request (newest event wins) — the partner does not
+define the order in which it applies items inside one call.
 
 Run tests: `python -m unittest discover -s tests`
 
